#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198083 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606" :question/problem "![](images/44/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1567255f-89d3-537d-9745-05c128c9a70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525249} {:answer/id #uuid "30323f71-005d-500f-ba38-9dce738cbeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525321} {:answer/id #uuid "3171223d-fda0-5492-85ec-2ca8aeb49434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525327} {:answer/id #uuid "34e7d47b-761b-582e-82d4-82f852906513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525339} {:answer/id #uuid "42a3398a-c116-593a-a2fb-eb35702dc483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3171223d-fda0-5492-85ec-2ca8aeb49434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525327} :answer-field/id #uuid "8707f64b-5a38-5067-b21a-113f373163a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7063b461-98a7-5c7e-afa4-7c0e6c07c12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18497" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4ae08104573cd8a96c47ae6d98993998622617c3dd932516055cd2b0cd4e55be.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the points on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x$ that are closest to the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =y+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006306fb-d1f5-5084-bc23-6f171183965a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525209} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee5f784-7760-5fc1-a06d-4af99980535d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525405} {:answer/id #uuid "901ba962-b8ce-529a-97dc-8257c3970528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525570} {:answer/id #uuid "e67b22cc-e319-5d6c-84b8-c400c13c7b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525777} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9fcd07-adf8-57e0-a429-dc973055ba61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "006306fb-d1f5-5084-bc23-6f171183965a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525209} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9194b8-ac65-503a-b901-f89939619d07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a9deff8-468e-536e-b35a-37a221ceafcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-18503" :question/problem "![](images/40/400427aa7dd8446124c44355959792d8e818e3118ee60ebb315c26a4b74375f1.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ and the point $(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-3(\\frac{3}{2})+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0ceca7-f868-55ca-ad65-7c8acc7b366a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525364} {:answer/id #uuid "53f09751-bead-5467-a0b4-c22d394e989f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525424} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6598a-e055-5352-92a9-e8cc9416b314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525625} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5a3f7-499e-591c-8e53-00ea9d85f8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525830} {:answer/id #uuid "fe99a76b-d948-50a7-8b29-d415a966079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6598a-e055-5352-92a9-e8cc9416b314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525625} :answer-field/id #uuid "332503ea-2f3b-5293-9831-cd53bab54fef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7941b15a-2aa3-5ad8-bfe5-4cdf148e4806" :question/math-academy-id "q-20073" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2ba745f9f5f495ae177be1c93926fc74dffab6e69b3035cda2e7ed273f4dd05.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-(\\frac{1}{2})+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2371624d-f92b-53aa-88b3-4ba724fd6c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525290} {:answer/id #uuid "7dede618-5fad-5037-878c-982ea9ea22eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525519} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9256dd-3b3e-53f5-bd72-9b2c32812111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525648} {:answer/id #uuid "eab19588-7c01-5347-9a19-69ffc09f36a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525787} {:answer/id #uuid "eb25181d-2b57-5a7f-a0d9-7560f0c671b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dede618-5fad-5037-878c-982ea9ea22eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525519} :answer-field/id #uuid "7723175c-a94c-5a00-bf6a-d5e0ab9a7482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e2d86f5-ab99-5ef8-9f2c-0675ddcc3d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-211283" :question/problem "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x}$ and the point $P(4,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+6x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-2(1)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1-2+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76ef0f93-55ce-5d9f-8c84-ab1d8ac24ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76ef0f93-55ce-5d9f-8c84-ab1d8ac24ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525505} :answer-field/id #uuid "898724a2-4a80-5adb-a3a5-35814a60b643" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bca0e45d-f143-5b0f-9307-2c5d5a1f757d" :question/math-academy-id "q-315643" :question/problem "![](images/14/144e610d70eaa52a62e4bbb2de2cd9dd9ed287a87a862d48f44cb7a161c7ee26.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+3$ and the point $(4,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=3$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(3)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-6(3)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593c398e-c867-598a-96ac-1514f5786e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "593c398e-c867-598a-96ac-1514f5786e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525434} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1cc86a0-17b1-5127-b7bc-ea453484905d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac677013-1927-5613-9941-c7a36ba6abf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344800" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b73cc737e5a07d6517431a41ad81d478e54c963b8295d4fb0996b6a33a3b34e.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$2x\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50164d43-e186-52a5-aa5d-03ce921970c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2(x-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50164d43-e186-52a5-aa5d-03ce921970c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2(x-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525408} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bdb2f7-bae7-5a47-bb42-7a7e0d61ce00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "444cffb1-de70-54cd-8f5f-c5135af025bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-344802" :question/problem "![](images/79/79033b7dda12c84d84f668a5e3a8eeb0be25e6ad9d7b34147826ef6bdcabdce3.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y={e}^{x-1}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$2x\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x-1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{x-1})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{2(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+{e}^{2(x-1)}\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2x+2{e}^{2(x-1)} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b9ac3a-e5d6-5e09-9565-8c91ff7e72d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\ln(x+2)}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95b9ac3a-e5d6-5e09-9565-8c91ff7e72d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\ln(x+2)}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525587} :answer-field/id #uuid "afacb823-875d-5437-9a23-fbc674a4d3af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e62d164e-09b5-5d2f-b961-352e1855d4f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-344809" :question/problem "![](images/19/19ab5199ec0848b87d5ccb276e7a78ac0c7284dafb2bdeb463c15732540d43ae.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$\\,\\,2x-2\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}} $=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69e9cf8-f3c2-542f-b8fc-de694b420589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69e9cf8-f3c2-542f-b8fc-de694b420589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525628} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b89023c-13ab-58a8-a5b6-e03c3d91e00c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3c46934-91bf-5aa2-b420-96168ea220bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-344817" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0bfd04a904f0619093e8b6d0103f1a03c82aa2b182693fa094238667e66dd2e.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{5x+4}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{5x+4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5x+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+5x+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+5x+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d8c9f8-6d13-5324-a8e0-da0c588fb23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d8c9f8-6d13-5324-a8e0-da0c588fb23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3e460a-f68f-56cd-9b72-a469666c469a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72e603ef-4823-5f36-9dda-6b01a7eedbc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344818" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x+7}+1$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x+7}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6x+7}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80aee408-07ed-5473-82c6-381df2606a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80aee408-07ed-5473-82c6-381df2606a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525526} :answer-field/id #uuid "243883fe-9e9f-5b1f-8e8f-e5cb649dc0b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5383eed-0cb1-58fc-ad37-136e38e3b0f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344825" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b68e9306032877d509bd8d0b38bac2dbf94ba4827ff501e200c51230442861b.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x$ that is closest to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8942aa9-0484-555f-8936-320feafc2bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8942aa9-0484-555f-8936-320feafc2bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525631} :answer-field/id #uuid "a67a7f3c-490f-5382-ace0-4fefcf0560be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8e4323d-cb33-53b2-a6a3-9708cfb8812c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344829" :question/problem "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x$ that is closest to the point $(2,-\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+8y+\\frac{65}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+8 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd94f47a-ff63-5519-845a-6b8fe23ffa21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd94f47a-ff63-5519-845a-6b8fe23ffa21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525835} :answer-field/id #uuid "02b51d52-be9b-59bb-bbfd-00a447bffaa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de1d317e-6601-5a5d-9bc7-8e4f2a0ea625" :question/math-academy-id "q-344847" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3$ and the point $P(2,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3-3)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}-8(2)+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8-16+9} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4020acf4-04e1-5b60-a2f7-a157b1314764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330520} {:answer/id #uuid "731eefef-20d5-574f-9043-1000fa8c1ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330987} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4ee1b1-d9b2-51c6-abc3-dc91b813f3ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{log}_{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331379} {:answer/id #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331700} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2baf64-fef0-5fb3-8a66-3f57f615f35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331700} :answer-field/id #uuid "497ee4c0-825b-5385-b6b4-603172d90ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0b5476f-0e78-5366-afe4-a0704f4f9240" :question/math-academy-id "q-49051" :question/problem "![](images/22/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21eea7e5-0174-531c-9582-360ddf9d9420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{-x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525284} {:answer/id #uuid "3cccd8eb-1ac8-5e98-a476-b1c9bb56c996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}\\cdot {3}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525357} {:answer/id #uuid "603b74f6-4b47-53ea-840a-6fe1f0caae7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{-x/2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525450} {:answer/id #uuid "63ac4a2a-1e2e-5cfe-9625-05473de939c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525459} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb641ee-6313-58d9-b66c-2afba73a6f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot {3}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb641ee-6313-58d9-b66c-2afba73a6f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot {3}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525760} :answer-field/id #uuid "7736b72f-d2f4-59b7-ba77-477d0d6c10dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f355b972-2cac-5b13-a407-9bcf1a157cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-87494" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d81bebc6bb5addfdd5f9006eba8cd4b5e5a13c6322495a6af3c5c02885ae6ee.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={3}^{-x/4}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{-x/4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({3}^{-x/4})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{3}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{-x/2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot {3}^{-x/2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
